\section{Composition Is Written as a Sequence of Words}

Concatenative languages compose operations by placing words next to one another. Forth combines this model with a data stack, return stack, dictionary, and frequently direct access to memory. Joy emphasizes quotation values and combinators. A conventional Forth definition is:

\begin{verbatim}
: SQUARE  ( n -- n2 )  DUP * ;
: SUMSQ   ( a b -- n ) SQUARE SWAP SQUARE + ;
3 4 SUMSQ .
\end{verbatim}

The parenthesized text is a stack-effect comment in many Forth systems, not necessarily a checked type declaration. \texttt{DUP} and \texttt{SWAP} make data routing explicit. Dictionary lookup, immediate words, compilation state, and user-defined words mean source parsing cannot be reduced to splitting a fixed opcode list. Forth's history and implementation tradition also include details beyond pure stack calculus \cite{moore1980forth}.

\section{Stack IR Is Not SSA with Names Erased}

A stack IR records an ordered instruction stream and an abstract stack shape at every boundary. A verifier can assign temporary value identities when simulating pushes and pops, then translate a well-formed basic block to SSA. At joins, however, predecessor stack heights and slot types must agree, and loop-carried slots need block arguments or phi-like merging. The source stack can also contain addresses and capabilities whose effects constrain reordering.

\Cref{fig:visual-373} follows \texttt{3 4 SUMSQ} through the abstract stack; the vertical order is semantic even though an SSA rendering would name values.

